## height
# THE CLIMB, written as one ribbon and the bank either side of it.  Every cell is authored: an
# unauthored cell is procedural and the smoothing pass would bridge the cliffs this map is made of.
#
# THE RIBBON.  Six near-level legs at z43 z36 z29 z22 z15 z8, 3-4 cells wide, joined by U-turns at
# the east edge (x17-20) and the west edge (x3-6).  The legs are FLAT and every voxel of the climb
# is spent in a U: 6 voxels over the 7 cells of the turn, 0.857 a cell, inside the one-voxel free
# step.  Leg heights 2, 8, 14, 20, 26, 32; the gate at 34.  The light drops a step at each turn.
#
# WHY IT CANNOT BE SHORT-CUT.  Off the ribbon the ground takes its height from the nearest LEG, not
# from the nearest point of the whole path, so the band between two legs is a single clean face of
#   6 voxels - 2.4 of cross-slope and roll = 3.6, rounding to 3 or 4.
# A walking step buys one voxel and a jump buys two, so three is a wall.  The only places the two
# legs come within two voxels of each other are the few cells just inside each bend, and those are
# field stones (## objects) — the shoulders of rock the bends turn around.  Walking bottom to top is
# about 145 cells; there is no line up the hillside shorter than the ribbon.
#
# THE WHISTLE LEDGE, x21-23 z22-24 at 16, off the OUTSIDE of the third bend.
#   verge  x20 z19-24 at 20 (walk-reachable off leg z22)
#   ledge  at 16: 20 - 16 = 4 voxels = TWO CELLS down.  A jump gains two, so there is no way back up
#          and the ledge is its own nav region — the map's ONE jump-only region.
#   far end x20-23 z25-26 at 13: 16 - 13 = 3 voxels down off the north... south end of the ledge,
#          then 13 -> 14 at z27, a walk edge onto the leg-29 bank.  Jump-only in, walked out.
#   isolation  N 4, W 4, S 3, E is the map edge.  Nothing else on the map is off the walk graph.
